WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  on the tarmac at aludaid air base in  qatar a c-17 opens its doors to a new  life  a three-hour journey that's taken days  of struggle  the aircraft packed with tales of fear  and hope  for one young man this evacuation flight  is a first daunting experience of flying  for the air crew they'll soon be off to  do it all again  this is a huge humanitarian undertaking  and the urgent nature of it  means it remains very fluid  there's no cap on how many flights like  this the qataris will send into  cardboard this week when they leave here  they don't know how many people they're  bringing back  it's simply as many as possible  for those left behind the window for  evacuating is closing fast  it's not clear how many flights are left  and the taliban have stopped people  traveling to the airport  one afghan security guard who worked for  g4s protecting the british embassy in  kabul until last year has lost all hope  of getting out  he told us the taliban is actively  looking for him they came to my father's  house and they had a room by room search  and they were looking for me for my  documents but they did not find anything  but luckily they got nothing and they  left here they abandoned us they are  leaving us behind g4s say they are  working with the uk government to  support a safe outcome for their  employees in afghanistan  this man who holds an australian  passport with relatives in the uk  was beaten on his way to try and get an  evacuation flight  it happened to me  and for the millions who aren't leaving  life is getting desperate  food prices are rising and people can't  access cash to buy it anywhere  since the taliban took control foreign  aid has been frozen  the world food programme has spoken to  sky news and warned they could run out  of funding as soon as next month  you can have unprecedented famine in  afghanistan  unprecedented famine  if  we don't receive the funds we need if  there's not an economic recovery plan  sooner than later because we already  know out of 40 million people 14 million  of them  are marching towards starvation  qatar is already mediating between the  taliban and afghan government officials  about what happens next with talks  underway on who will take over running  kabul airport  they're also a key player in the  humanitarian crisis  accommodation built for qatar's world  cup is now housing refugees  the person at the heart of this effort  is the country's deputy foreign minister  we didn't expect  this number of  babies kids pregnant women  and we're trying to cope with the  situation  everyone was talking about a timeline  that would last for weeks or months  but then overnight everyone was  surprised that  you know what happened has happened and  we need to deal with the situation  and with remaining evacuations now  numbered  international efforts must focus on  helping the people left behind  sally lockwood sky news doha

